WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.371
Beetlejuice.

00:00:01.371 --> 00:00:02.162
Beetlejuice.

00:00:02.162 --> 00:00:03.629
Beetle…popcorn?!

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:08.520
Tim Burton had a great time in the Addams
Family world, and you probably missed this

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.099
sneaky Beetlejuice Easter egg.

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:15.380
Netflix's runaway success, "Wednesday," has
a lot of secrets.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.960
Based on the Addams Family daughter of the
same name, played by Jenna Ortega, "Wednesday"

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.220
places the iconic character into Nevermore
Academy, a school that holds a special place

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.269
in the history of the spooky family on account
of Gomez and Morticia having met there.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:33.550
Of course, one of the best people to bring
this new "Wednesday" show to the small silver

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.610
screen is the legendary Tim Burton.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:41.379
Made famous for his endeavors like "Edward
Scissorhands," "Batman," and "Beetlejuice,"

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:45.980
Burton has a rather distinct style that is
almost immediately apparent to anybody who

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.831
has had any previous experiences with the
director.

00:00:48.831 --> 00:00:50.927
"Wait'll they get a load of me."

00:00:50.927 --> 00:00:56.000
Speaking with Empire, Tim Burton spoke about
what inspired him to take on "Wednesday" and explained,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.549
Tim Burton and the Addams Family is definitely
an interesting combination.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.090
And considering the status of Burton's works
in pop culture, it's no surprise that "Wednesday"

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:19.950
is filled to the brim with clever callbacks
and nods to his other movies.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.820
Throughout "Wednesday," there are tons of
Easter eggs inspired by Tim Burton's other

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.790
famous works, which was very intentional.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.670
Production designer Mark Scruton delighted
in filling scenes with homages to Burton's

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:31.770
body of work.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.189
One particular location in "Wednesday" that's
absolutely adorned with Burton references

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:39.990
is the Weathervanes coffee shop that many
Nevermore Academy students frequent.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.610
If you pay close attention, you can see that
the weathervanes channel the essence of two

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.210
Burton movies — one is inspired by the Headless
Horseman from "Sleepy Hollow," and one emulates

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.149
the hat from "Charlie and the Chocolate Factory."

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:56.909
However, these two examples were far from
the only Easter eggs, and there were several

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.039
that were explicitly designed to reference
previous "Addams Family" shows and movies,

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:04.979
like Pilgrim World and the archery scene,
which are nods to Camp Chippewa in "Addams

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:09.020
Family Values" and Wednesday's well-known
affinity for archery.

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:11.639
"It's an American bald eagle."

00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:13.667
"But aren't they extinct?"

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.000
"They are now."

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.530
And the secret way into Nevermore Academy's
secret society consists of the two finger

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.500
snaps made popular by the "Addams Family"
theme song.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.340
Of course, one cannot put Burton-based Easter
eggs in "Wednesday" without some involving

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.180
the "Ghost with the Most."

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.880
Released in 1988, "Beetlejuice" tells the
story of a recently deceased couple, played

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:38.120
by Alec Baldwin and Geena Davis, who has to
come to terms with their status as ghosts

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.459
and the family that moves into their house.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:44.550
Seeking to restore their former peace and
ownership of their home, the previously mentioned

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.220
couple conjures up a bio-exorcist known as
Beetlejuice.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.900
Considering the lasting popularity of "Beetlejuice"
among Tim Burton's fans, it makes perfect

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.195
sense as to why there are multiple "Beetlejuice"
references in "Wednesday."

00:02:58.195 --> 00:02:59.935
"You bunch'a losers.

00:03:00.707 --> 00:03:03.049
You're working with a professional here."

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:07.520
Probably the easiest of these references is
the shrunken head that resembles the big game

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.019
hunter in the afterlife waiting room from
"Beetlejuice" upon the desk of Principal Weems.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:16.310
However, there is a more subtle reference
in "Wednesday" that emulates the famous white

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.380
and black striped suit made popular by Beetlejuice.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.540
In Episode 7, titled "If You Don't Woe Me
by Now," Wednesday and Tyler watch the supposed

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.040
horror movie "Legally Blonde" while eating
popcorn at Crackstone's Crypt.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.379
However, eagle-eyed viewers or those with
their brightness settings turned up may notice

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:38.300
that the popcorn bag has the same color scheme
and look as Beetlejuice's typical suit, which

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.920
was definitely intentional.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.739
Luckily for Wednesday and Tyler, they weren't
immediately compelled to sing "Day-O!," though

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:47.693
this is still a great Easter egg for longtime
fans of Burton.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.710
"Yeah."

